1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,319
This walkthrough shows how Portage uses the Model
Context Protocol. We will connect Sora to

2
00:00:05,319 --> 00:00:10,638
Parallel web search, inspect a real tool call, and
control which external tools Sora can

3
00:00:10,638 --> 00:00:10,993
use.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,916
Portage has two MCP roles. As a client, it calls
external MCP servers for Sora.

5
00:00:17,916 --> 00:00:23,832
As a server, it exposes Portage tools to external
assistants, using standard input and output.

6
00:00:23,832 --> 00:00:25,409
These are separate connections.

7
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:34,433
In Settings, select MCP server. The external server
named parallel search MCP connects to search

8
00:00:34,433 --> 00:00:41,067
dot parallel dot A I, over Streamable HTTP.
Connected means Portage successfully discovered its
tools.

9
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:48,259
Click Web Search to inspect its description.
Parallel provides Web Search for internet queries
and

10
00:00:48,259 --> 00:00:53,617
Web Fetch for retrieving pages. Portage exposes
tools that pass its read only policy. A

11
00:00:53,617 --> 00:00:57,190
connection test discovers tools; it does not run a
search.

12
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:04,187
Now ask Sora to search the web. Portage selects the
available search tool, sends the

13
00:01:04,187 --> 00:01:09,374
arguments to Parallel, and passes the returned
results back to the model. The tool activity

14
00:01:09,374 --> 00:01:12,486
in the conversation identifies the server and tool
used.

15
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:27,833
The check mark records the completed external tool
call. The response below is generated from

16
00:01:27,833 --> 00:01:32,965
the live search results. Use this activity trace to
distinguish an actual search from a

17
00:01:32,965 --> 00:01:34,334
model answering from memory.

18
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:41,171
Turn off Web Search to remove it from Sora on its
next message. The setting

19
00:01:41,171 --> 00:01:46,142
is saved immediately. Web Fetch stays enabled, so
you can control each tool independently without

20
00:01:46,142 --> 00:01:47,137
deleting the server.

21
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:53,827
Turn Web Search back on to make it available again.
The server level Available to

22
00:01:53,827 --> 00:01:58,754
Sora switch controls every tool from this server.
The individual tool switches give you finer

23
00:01:58,754 --> 00:01:59,082
control.

24
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:06,373
The Connect an MCP client section shows
configuration for Visual Studio Code and other
clients.

25
00:02:06,373 --> 00:02:12,146
The client starts the Portage MCP server as a local
process. Portage tools support your

26
00:02:12,146 --> 00:02:15,995
migration workflow, and proposed changes still
require approval in Portage.

27
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:22,945
You have seen live Parallel tool discovery, a real
web search through Sora, individual tool

28
00:02:22,945 --> 00:02:28,790
controls, and configuration for connecting external
MCP clients to Portage. Choose your servers, inspect
their

29
00:02:28,790 --> 00:02:31,907
tools, and enable only what your team needs.
